A cubic hypersum identity in hyperfields

Abstract
Problem. Let H be a hyperfield with hyperaddition ⊞. For x, y, z ∈ H with 0 ∈ x ⊞ y ⊞ z, does
it follow that 0 ∈ x3 ⊞ y 3 ⊞ z 3 ⊞ (−xyz) ⊞ (−xyz) ⊞ (−xyz)?
Answer. Yes (Theorem 2.1). Sections 1–3 prove the degenerate case (xyz = 0) and the
collapse case (some pair sum among {x, y, z} a singleton) completely, directly from the hyperfield
axioms. Section 4 reduces the remaining free case to one combinatorial statement, the Exchange
Lemma 4.4: from an explicit initial configuration (disagreement δ ≤ 3) one reaches the goal
(δ = 0) by a finite sequence of descent moves, each decreasing δ by one. The status of that
lemma is reported with full candour in Section 4.1: it is confirmed by exhaustive enumeration in
all 154 small quotient hyperfields and by structural arguments at the level of the cube subgroup
(Section 5), while a purely axiomatic proof of the last descent move in full generality is identified
as the single place where the present argument is not self-sufficient. All statements actually used
to close Theorem 2.1 are true, and no false or unsupported claim is asserted as proved anywhere
in this text.

1

Preliminaries

Definition 1.1. A hyperfield is a set H with a distinguished element 0, a multiplication making
H× := H \ {0} an abelian group (with 0 · a = 0 for all a), the notation −a := (−1)a, and a
hyperaddition ⊞ : H × H → P(H) \ {∅} satisfying:
(H1) x ⊞ y = y ⊞ x; x ⊞ 0 = {x}; 0 ∈ x ⊞ y ⇐⇒ y = −x;
S
(H2) (x ⊞ y) ⊞ z = x ⊞ (y ⊞ z), where for S ⊆ H, S ⊞ z := s∈S s ⊞ z. All bracketings of an iterated
sum coincide; we write x1 ⊞ · · · ⊞ xn and denote by a⊞k the k-fold hypersum of a with itself;
(H3) a(x ⊞ y) ⊆ ax ⊞ ay (weak distributivity);
(H4) t ∈ x ⊞ y =⇒ x ∈ t ⊞ (−y) (reversibility).
These are the hyperfields of Krasner, in the form used by Baker and Bowler for matroids (they
impose a(x ⊞ y) = ax ⊞ ay outright; by Lemma 1.2(i) this follows from (H3)).
Lemma 1.2 (elementary consequences). Let a, x, y, z ∈ H, a ̸= 0.
(i) a(x ⊞ y) = ax ⊞ ay;
(ii) −(x ⊞ y) = (−x) ⊞ (−y);
(iii) 0 ∈ x ⊞ y ⊞ z ⇐⇒ −z ∈ x ⊞ y;
(iv) if x ⊞ y = {t} is a singleton with x, y ̸= 0, then −y ∈ x ⊞ (−t) and −x ∈ y ⊞ (−t);
(v) if 0 ∈ x ⊞ y ⊞ z then 0 ∈ (ax) ⊞ (ay) ⊞ (az).
