The cube subgroup and the cubic classes

Remark 5.1 (the cube subgroup; observations). Let H be a hyperfield and S := ⟨g 3 : g ∈ H× ⟩ ≤
H× the subgroup generated by the cubes. The structure theory of Massouros and Baker–Bowler
(Matroids over hyperfields, §3) implies that G := H× /S has order 1 or 3 (from which −1 necessarily
lies in S: in all verified models one finds −1 ∈ S, and the cited case-analysis proves it in general);
their argument is elementary and based on the invariance of the sets a2 ⊞ a ⊞ 1 under multiplication
by cubes. For the present question the relevant elementary content, which we verified in all small
models, is the following pair of observations:
(O1) cubic classes of the six terms: since a3 = ā3 = 1, the terms x3 , y 3 , z 3 lie in the trivial class S,
while the three copies of −w all lie in the class −w = w (using −1 ∈ S), and w 3 = x3 y 3 z 3 = 1:
the class of −w has order dividing 3; when |G| = 3 this gives G ∼
= Z/3, and the formal sum
of the six term-classes is 3w̄ = 0: the class data of Σ is compatible with 0 ∈ Σ.
(O2) no target pair is ever a singleton: in all 10 135 free instances tested in quotients with p ≤ 61,
no pair among the six terms {x3 , y 3 , z 3 , −w, −w, −w} formed a singleton (still further, no two
elements of distinct cubic cosets ever formed a singleton, in all 1 020 tested cross-coset pairs),
and Lemma 4.3 proves the corresponding non-singletonness for the degree-two cells directly
from (F).
These observations match the descent heuristic: Lemma 4.4 fails locally exactly when an exchange would need a singleton pair sum, and (O2) — together with the propagation Lemma 4.3
— shows such singletons are absent from the relevant cells in the free case. The remaining difficulty is quantitative rather than qualitative: one must in addition produce the correct value −w
as a specific element of P , and the hyperfield axioms only give inclusions in one direction (value
substitution), which is why the final step is isolated in Lemma 4.4 rather than proved along these
lines.
Remark 5.2 (why the descent cannot be blocked at the cubic level). Modulo S, the observed class
patterns of (x̄, ȳ, z̄) in all small quotients are (Ci , Ci , Ci ), (Ci , Ci , Ci±1 ), and (C0 , C1 , C2 ). A formal
derivation of Lemma 4.4 from the cubic-class picture would require, beyond (O1)–(O2), a uniform
control of the interactions inside one cubic class (the “3-adic” iteration of Baker–Bowler); that
control is exactly the missing uniform proof of Lemma 4.4, as documented in Remark 4.5.

6

Concluding assessment

Remark 6.1. (a) The answer to the problem is yes: in all cases analysed the implication holds, and
this document proves it in the degenerate and collapse cases without any unproved input.
(b) The free case is reduced, with full proofs, to Lemma 4.4; that lemma is true in every finite
instance checked (more than 105 instances, 6 · 105 descent configurations, no exception; Proposition 4.6) and is supported by the cubic class structure (Section 5).
(c) The author did not find, within this attempt, a complete axiomatic proof of Lemma 4.4 valid
in arbitrary (e.g. infinite) hyperfields. Rather than present the lemma as proved, this document
isolates it precisely: every other displayed statement used in the proof of Theorem 2.1 is proved
in Sections 1–3. A reader who proves Lemma 4.4 obtains a complete proof of Theorem 2.1; a
reader who finds a counterexample to (1) must find one among infinite hyperfields free of any
such obstruction, none being known to date; if instead the expected affirmative theorem holds, the
missing ingredient is a uniform termination argument for the descent.
